# Visual Use Case Diagrams - PlantUML

This file contains PlantUML diagrams for key use cases in the E-Commerce Microservices Platform.

## 1. Order Checkout Saga Flow (Choreography)

```plantuml
@startuml Order Checkout Saga
!theme plain

actor Customer
participant "API Gateway" as Gateway
participant "Order Service" as Order
participant "Inventory Service" as Inventory
participant "Payment Service" as Payment
participant "Shipping Service" as Shipping
participant "Notification Service" as Notification
queue "Message Broker" as Broker

Customer -> Gateway: POST /checkout
Gateway -> Order: Create Order
Order -> Order: Create Order (PENDING)
Order -> Order: Write to Outbox
Order -> Broker: Publish OrderCreated
Order --> Gateway: OrderId
Gateway --> Customer: Order Confirmation

Broker -> Inventory: OrderCreated Event
Inventory -> Inventory: Reserve Stock
alt Inventory Available
    Inventory -> Inventory: Update Stock
    Inventory -> Broker: Publish InventoryReserved
else Insufficient Stock
    Inventory -> Broker: Publish InventoryReservationFailed
    Broker -> Order: InventoryReservationFailed
    Order -> Order: Cancel Order
    Order --> Customer: Order Cancelled (Out of Stock)
end

Broker -> Payment: InventoryReserved Event
Payment -> Payment: Process Payment
alt Payment Success
    Payment -> Payment: Charge Customer
    Payment -> Broker: Publish PaymentCompleted
else Payment Failed
    Payment -> Broker: Publish PaymentFailed
    Broker -> Inventory: PaymentFailed Event
    Inventory -> Inventory: Release Reservation
    Inventory -> Broker: Publish InventoryReleased
    Broker -> Order: PaymentFailed Event
    Order -> Order: Update Status (PAYMENT_FAILED)
    Order -> Broker: Publish OrderCancelled
end

Broker -> Order: PaymentCompleted Event
Order -> Order: Update Status (CONFIRMED)
Order -> Broker: Publish OrderConfirmed

Broker -> Shipping: OrderConfirmed Event
Shipping -> Shipping: Create Shipment
Shipping -> Broker: Publish ShipmentCreated

Broker -> Notification: OrderCreated, PaymentCompleted, ShipmentCreated
Notification -> Customer: Send Notifications

@enduml
```

## 2. Complete Order State Machine

```plantuml
@startuml Order State Machine
!theme plain

[*] --> PENDING : Order Created

PENDING --> INVENTORY_RESERVED : Inventory Reserved
PENDING --> CANCELLED : Inventory Reservation Failed

INVENTORY_RESERVED --> CONFIRMED : Payment Completed
INVENTORY_RESERVED --> PAYMENT_FAILED : Payment Failed
PAYMENT_FAILED --> CANCELLED : Compensation Completed

CONFIRMED --> PROCESSING : Shipment Created
PROCESSING --> SHIPPED : Package Dispatched
SHIPPED --> DELIVERED : Package Delivered

PENDING --> CANCELLED : Customer Cancels
CONFIRMED --> CANCELLING : Customer Requests Cancel
PROCESSING --> CANCELLING : Customer Requests Cancel
CANCELLING --> CANCELLED : Refund & Compensation Done

DELIVERED --> [*]
CANCELLED --> [*]

@enduml
```

## 3. Payment Service Integration

```plantuml
@startuml Payment Service Flow
!theme plain

actor Customer
participant "Order Service" as Order
participant "Payment Service" as Payment
participant "Stripe Provider" as Stripe
participant "VNPay Provider" as VNPay
queue "Message Broker" as Broker
database "Payment DB" as DB

Order -> Broker: Publish InitiatePayment
Broker -> Payment: InitiatePayment Event
Payment -> Payment: Check Idempotency
Payment -> DB: Create Payment (PENDING)

Customer -> Payment: Submit Payment Details
Payment -> Payment: Select Provider
alt Credit Card (Stripe)
    Payment -> Stripe: Process Payment
    Stripe --> Payment: Transaction Result
else E-Wallet (VNPay)
    Payment -> VNPay: Generate Payment URL
    VNPay --> Payment: Payment URL
    Payment --> Customer: Redirect to VNPay
    Customer -> VNPay: Authorize Payment
    VNPay -> Payment: Webhook Callback
end

alt Payment Success
    Payment -> DB: Update Payment (COMPLETED)
    Payment -> Broker: Publish PaymentCompleted
else Payment Failed
    Payment -> DB: Update Payment (FAILED)
    Payment -> Broker: Publish PaymentFailed
end

@enduml
```

## 4. Inventory Reservation with Locking

```plantuml
@startuml Inventory Reservation
!theme plain

participant "Order Service" as Order
queue "Message Broker" as Broker
participant "Inventory Service" as Inventory
database "Inventory DB" as DB

Order -> Broker: Publish OrderCreated
Broker -> Inventory: OrderCreated Event

Inventory -> Inventory: Check Idempotency
Inventory -> DB: BEGIN TRANSACTION

loop For Each Item
    Inventory -> DB: SELECT ... FOR UPDATE (Lock Row)
    Inventory -> DB: Check Available >= Requested
    alt Sufficient Stock
        Inventory -> DB: Available -= Quantity
        Inventory -> DB: Reserved += Quantity
        Inventory -> DB: Create Reservation Record
    else Insufficient Stock
        Inventory -> DB: ROLLBACK
        Inventory -> Broker: Publish InventoryReservationFailed
    end
end

Inventory -> DB: COMMIT TRANSACTION
Inventory -> DB: Store EventId (Idempotency)
Inventory -> Broker: Publish InventoryReserved

@enduml
```

## 5. Outbox Pattern Implementation

```plantuml
@startuml Outbox Pattern
!theme plain

participant "Order Service" as Service
database "Order DB" as DB
participant "Outbox Publisher" as Publisher
queue "Message Broker" as Broker

Service -> DB: BEGIN TRANSACTION
Service -> DB: INSERT INTO Orders (...)
Service -> DB: INSERT INTO Outbox (EventId, EventType, Payload)
Service -> DB: COMMIT TRANSACTION

note right of DB
  Both order and event
  saved atomically
end note

Publisher -> DB: Poll Outbox Table
DB --> Publisher: Unprocessed Events

loop For Each Event
    Publisher -> Broker: Publish Event
    Broker --> Publisher: ACK
    Publisher -> DB: UPDATE Outbox SET ProcessedAt = NOW()
end

note right of Publisher
  Background job runs
  every 5 seconds
end note

@enduml
```

## 6. API Gateway Request Flow

```plantuml
@startuml API Gateway Flow
!theme plain

actor Client
participant "API Gateway" as Gateway
participant "Redis Cache" as Redis
participant "Identity Service" as Identity
participant "Product Service" as Product

Client -> Gateway: GET /api/products/123\nAuthorization: Bearer {token}

Gateway -> Gateway: Extract JWT Token
Gateway -> Gateway: Validate Token Signature
Gateway -> Identity: Validate Token
Identity --> Gateway: User Claims

Gateway -> Gateway: Check Authorization
Gateway -> Gateway: Generate Correlation ID
Gateway -> Gateway: Apply Rate Limiting

Gateway -> Redis: Check Cache
alt Cache Hit
    Redis --> Gateway: Cached Response
    Gateway --> Client: 200 OK (X-Cache: HIT)
else Cache Miss
    Gateway -> Product: GET /products/123\nX-User-Id: 456\nX-Correlation-Id: abc123
    Product --> Gateway: Product Data
    Gateway -> Redis: Store in Cache (TTL: 5min)
    Gateway --> Client: 200 OK (X-Cache: MISS)
end

Gateway -> Gateway: Log Request/Response

@enduml
```

## 7. Search Service Event-Driven Indexing

```plantuml
@startuml Search Indexing
!theme plain

participant "Product Service" as Product
database "Product DB" as ProductDB
queue "Message Broker" as Broker
participant "Search Service" as Search
database "Elasticsearch" as ES

Product -> ProductDB: Update Product Price
Product -> ProductDB: INSERT INTO Outbox
ProductDB --> Product: Success

Broker -> Search: ProductUpdated Event
Search -> Search: Check Idempotency
Search -> Search: Transform to Search Document
Search -> ES: Update Document
ES --> Search: Indexed

Search -> Search: Store EventId
note right of Search
  Search index eventually
  consistent with Product DB
end note

@enduml
```

## 8. Circuit Breaker Pattern

```plantuml
@startuml Circuit Breaker States
!theme plain

state "CLOSED" as Closed {
    Closed : All requests pass through
    Closed : Track failure metrics
}

state "OPEN" as Open {
    Open : Reject requests immediately
    Open : Return fallback response
    Open : Wait for timeout
}

state "HALF_OPEN" as HalfOpen {
    HalfOpen : Allow limited requests
    HalfOpen : Test if service recovered
}

[*] --> Closed

Closed --> Open : Failure rate > threshold\n(e.g., 50% in 1 min)
Open --> HalfOpen : After timeout\n(e.g., 30 seconds)
HalfOpen --> Closed : Trial requests succeed
HalfOpen --> Open : Trial requests fail

note right of Open
  Prevents cascading failures
  Allows service to recover
end note

@enduml
```

## 9. Saga Orchestration Pattern

```plantuml
@startuml Saga Orchestrator
!theme plain

participant "Order Service\n(Orchestrator)" as Orchestrator
participant "Inventory Service" as Inventory
participant "Payment Service" as Payment
participant "Shipping Service" as Shipping
database "Saga State DB" as SagaDB

Orchestrator -> SagaDB: Create Saga Instance
Orchestrator -> SagaDB: State: STARTED

Orchestrator -> Inventory: ReserveInventoryCommand
Inventory --> Orchestrator: InventoryReserved
Orchestrator -> SagaDB: Update: inventory_reserved

Orchestrator -> Payment: ProcessPaymentCommand
alt Payment Success
    Payment --> Orchestrator: PaymentCompleted
    Orchestrator -> SagaDB: Update: payment_completed
    
    Orchestrator -> Shipping: CreateShipmentCommand
    Shipping --> Orchestrator: ShipmentCreated
    Orchestrator -> SagaDB: State: COMPLETED
else Payment Failed
    Payment --> Orchestrator: PaymentFailed
    Orchestrator -> SagaDB: State: COMPENSATING
    
    Orchestrator -> Inventory: ReleaseInventoryCommand (Compensate)
    Inventory --> Orchestrator: InventoryReleased
    
    Orchestrator -> SagaDB: State: COMPENSATED
end

@enduml
```

## 10. Distributed Tracing Flow

```plantuml
@startuml Distributed Tracing
!theme plain

actor Customer
participant "API Gateway" as Gateway
participant "Order Service" as Order
participant "Inventory Service" as Inventory
participant "Payment Service" as Payment
participant "Jaeger" as Tracer

Customer -> Gateway: POST /orders
Gateway -> Gateway: Generate TraceId: abc-123\nSpanId: span-1
Gateway -> Tracer: Start Span (gateway)

Gateway -> Order: Headers:\n  traceparent: abc-123-span-1
Order -> Order: Extract Trace Context\nCreate Child Span: span-2
Order -> Tracer: Start Span (order-service)

Order -> Inventory: Headers:\n  traceparent: abc-123-span-2
Inventory -> Inventory: Extract Trace Context\nCreate Child Span: span-3
Inventory -> Tracer: Start Span (inventory-service)
Inventory --> Order: Response
Inventory -> Tracer: End Span (inventory-service)

Order -> Payment: Headers:\n  traceparent: abc-123-span-2
Payment -> Payment: Extract Trace Context\nCreate Child Span: span-4
Payment -> Tracer: Start Span (payment-service)
Payment --> Order: Response
Payment -> Tracer: End Span (payment-service)

Order --> Gateway: Response
Order -> Tracer: End Span (order-service)

Gateway --> Customer: Response
Gateway -> Tracer: End Span (gateway)

note right of Tracer
  Complete trace shows:
  Gateway (10ms)
    └─ Order (250ms)
       ├─ Inventory (40ms)
       └─ Payment (200ms)
end note

@enduml
```

## 11. Review & Rating Aggregation

```plantuml
@startuml Review Rating Update
!theme plain

actor Customer
participant "Review Service" as Review
database "Review DB" as ReviewDB
queue "Message Broker" as Broker
participant "Product Service" as Product

Customer -> Review: POST /reviews\n{rating: 5, comment: "Great!"}
Review -> ReviewDB: Create Review (PENDING_MODERATION)
Review -> Review: Auto-moderate

alt Approved
    Review -> ReviewDB: Update Status = APPROVED
    Review -> Broker: Publish ReviewApproved\n{productId: 123, rating: 5}
    
    Broker -> Review: ReviewApproved Event
    Review -> ReviewDB: Recalculate Aggregate
    Review -> ReviewDB: UPDATE ProductRatings\nSET TotalReviews += 1,\n    RatingSum += 5,\n    AvgRating = RatingSum/TotalReviews
    
    Review -> Broker: Publish ProductRatingUpdated
    
    Broker -> Product: ProductRatingUpdated Event
    Product -> Product: Invalidate Product Cache
else Rejected
    Review -> ReviewDB: Update Status = REJECTED
    Review --> Customer: Review Rejected (reason)
end

@enduml
```

## 12. Notification Multi-Channel Flow

```plantuml
@startuml Notification Service
!theme plain

queue "Message Broker" as Broker
participant "Notification Service" as Notification
database "Notification DB" as DB
participant "Email Provider\n(SendGrid)" as Email
participant "SMS Provider\n(Twilio)" as SMS
participant "Push Provider\n(Firebase)" as Push
actor Customer

Broker -> Notification: OrderCreated Event
Notification -> Notification: Check Idempotency
Notification -> DB: Retrieve Customer Preferences

alt Email Enabled
    Notification -> Email: Send Order Confirmation
    Email --> Notification: Sent
    Notification -> DB: Log Email Sent
end

alt SMS Enabled (Express Orders)
    Notification -> SMS: Send SMS
    SMS --> Notification: Sent
    Notification -> DB: Log SMS Sent
end

alt Push Enabled
    Notification -> Push: Send Push Notification
    Push --> Notification: Sent
    Notification -> DB: Log Push Sent
end

Notification -> DB: Store EventId (Idempotency)

Email -> Customer: Order Confirmation Email
SMS -> Customer: SMS Notification
Push -> Customer: Push Notification

@enduml
```

## Usage Instructions

To render these diagrams:

1. **Online**: Use [PlantUML Online Server](http://www.plantuml.com/plantuml/uml/)
2. **VS Code**: Install "PlantUML" extension
3. **IntelliJ**: Built-in PlantUML support
4. **Command Line**: 
   ```bash
   java -jar plantuml.jar diagrams.puml
   ```

5. **Export as Images**:
   ```bash
   plantuml -tpng diagrams.puml
   plantuml -tsvg diagrams.puml
   ```

